e/math :answer/value "-6<{f}^{-1}(x)\\le -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16b17b34-ef25-57ed-8984-0c7a54f507e0" :answer/id #uuid "16b17b34-ef25-57ed-8984-0c7a54f507e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\le {f}^{-1}(x)<4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a80e40f5-874e-56ab-80fe-9d43a174a171" :question/id #uuid "a80e40f5-874e-56ab-80fe-9d43a174a171" :question/math-academy-id "q-7027" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)$ is $x\\ge 4$ and the range of $f$ is $f(x)\\ge 2.$ What is the range of the inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is:\n\n$$\nx\\ge 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 4\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd345aa7-7bbc-5fb0-a28b-bbb1afa1e0e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd345aa7-7bbc-5fb0-a28b-bbb1afa1e0e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd345aa7-7bbc-5fb0-a28b-bbb1afa1e0e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-26720517-4d72-5460-b56e-b19365511cf0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc5cc71d-222e-5763-9153-94ad33d3fa3a" "answer-bf83acff-61f2-538c-8a3c-3655ca48cc76" "answer-a360d025-c375-5503-9d95-becd0af97805" "answer-ccbb1c10-5851-56e1-8bd3-1e66ddc4a54f" "answer-26720517-4d72-5460-b56e-b19365511cf0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc5cc71d-222e-5763-9153-94ad33d3fa3a" :answer/id #uuid "cc5cc71d-222e-5763-9153-94ad33d3fa3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\le {f}^{-1}(x)\\le 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf83acff-61f2-538c-8a3c-3655ca48cc76" :answer/id #uuid "bf83acff-61f2-538c-8a3c-3655ca48cc76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2<{f}^{-1}(x)<4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a360d025-c375-5503-9d95-becd0af97805" :answer/id #uuid "a360d025-c375-5503-9d95-becd0af97805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\le {f}^{-1}(x)\\le 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ccbb1c10-5851-56e1-8bd3-1e66ddc4a54f" :answer/id #uuid "ccbb1c10-5851-56e1-8bd3-1e66ddc4a54f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-26720517-4d72-5460-b56e-b19365511cf0" :answer/id #uuid "26720517-4d72-5460-b56e-b19365511cf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)\\ge 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-68db7057-3186-55cb-a475-e68617c8e05d" :question/id #uuid "68db7057-3186-55cb-a475-e68617c8e05d" :question/math-academy-id "q-108477" :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)$ is $-1\\le x\\le 9$ and the range of $f$ is $-2\\le f(x)\\le 11.$ What is the domain of the inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nThe range of $f(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-2\\le f(x)\\le 11\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-2\\le x\\le 11\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc2e91fa-233b-5f55-9b5d-8a3d23d9b72f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc2e91fa-233b-5f55-9b5d-8a3d23d9b72f" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2e91fa-233b-5f55-9b5d-8a3d23d9b72f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e12067e2-5fe8-5678-823b-e88909af3615" "answer-2a8403c3-9430-52e7-946d-dcfca1e36081" "answer-3dd83ed2-4a6b-5d6c-bbc0-0f8245736c3a" "answer-f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" "answer-70b3347f-c4e0-5cbf-b7bb-0f2bc1f18395"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12067e2-5fe8-5678-823b-e88909af3615" :answer/id #uuid "e12067e2-5fe8-5678-823b-e88909af3615" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a8403c3-9430-52e7-946d-dcfca1e36081" :answer/id #uuid "2a8403c3-9430-52e7-946d-dcfca1e36081" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3dd83ed2-4a6b-5d6c-bbc0-0f8245736c3a" :answer/id #uuid "3dd83ed2-4a6b-5d6c-bbc0-0f8245736c3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le -1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" :answer/id #uuid "f9c684f0-2711-5edf-8cff-d6913f105ea3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70b3347f-c4e0-5cbf-b7bb-0f2bc1f18395" :answer/id #uuid "70b3347f-c4e0-5cbf-b7bb-0f2bc1f18395" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186180753 :tutorial/content "Consider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/c0/c0bd305d326688f49ecf0579ee42217a58a7521be8a410f619cbe26f5c443991.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n- the domain of $f(x)$ is $[1,2],$ and\n\n- the range of $f(x)$ is $[2,6].$\n\nRecall that each point $(a,b)$ on the graph of ${\\color{blue}y=f(x)}$ has a corresponding point $(b,a)$ on the graph of the inverse function ${\\color{red}y=f^{-1}(x)}.$ That is to say, the $x$ and $y$ coordinates swap.\n\n![](images/1d/1dd83a834e14f8f3679db1c013f23cce80ec9b2546393cf77ee613140c6b7b5f.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- the domain of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[2,6],$ and\n\n- the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is $[1,2].$\n\nIn general:\n\n- The domain of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the range of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}.$\n\n- The range of a function ${\\color{blue}f(x)}$ is the domain of its inverse function ${\\color{red}f^{-1}(x)}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186179882 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186179422 :question/problem "For the function $y = f(x)$ shown below, what is the domain of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)?$\n\n![](images/ad/adf44ae3f3c8cb3bbe1958cca12e559b68d4d95247d087c69cb90425dbc05785.png)"] [17592186179422 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the range of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq f(x) \\lt 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 9.\n$$"] [17592186179424 :question/problem "![](images/c1/c187a2fdc4c34135836afd2f28af17776cee6408b1e7bf3124d4833ca0529000.png)\n\nFor the function $y = f(x)$ shown above, what is the range of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)?$"] [17592186179424 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-1 \\lt x \\lt 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-1 \\lt f^{-1}(x) \\lt 4.\n$$"] [17592186179423 :question/problem "The domain of the function $f(x)$ is $-3\\lt x \\leq 3$ and the range of $f$ is $-2\\leq f(x) \\lt 2.$ What is the range of the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)?$"] [17592186179423 :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function $f^{-1}(x).$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3\\lt x \\leq 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n-3\\lt  f^{-1}(x) \\leq 3.\n$$"] ["question-177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f" :question/id #uuid "177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f"] ["question-177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-155964"] ["question-177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f" :question/problem "![](images/42/4297165a04f0e68c0f1d1b59d39643734e11bcd1809bf1946e4c3b67bee3a601.png)\n\nFor the function $y=f(x)$ shown above, what is the domain of the inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x)?$"] ["question-177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following:\n\n- The domain of a function $f(x)$ is the range of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n- The range of a function $f(x)$ is the domain of its inverse function ${f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the range of $f(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-1<f(x)\\le 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n-1<x\\le 3\n$$"] ["question-177999f4-8826-5bd4-8370-aadea8a26d3f" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-3080165f-6478-50dd-b12c-9ef47d4dc9ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "3080165f-6478-50dd-b12c-9ef47d4dc9ce"] ["answer-a04b075d-3871-5cba-afd7-8a83a53b115b" :answer/id #uuid "a04b075d-3871-5cba-afd7-8a83a53b115b"] ["answer-a04b075d-3871-5cba-afd7-8a83a53b115b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a04b075d-3871-5cba-afd7-8a83a53b115b" :answer/value "-1<x<3"] ["answer-667a998f-6463-5da3-8065-7e6a8ea8a6ce" :answer/id #uuid "667a998f-6463-5da3-8065-7e6a8ea8a6ce"] ["answer-667a998f-6463-5da3-8065-7e6a8ea8a6ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-667a998f-6463-5da3-8065-7e6a8ea8a6ce" :answer/value "-1<x\\le 3"] ["answer-9abfb29c-8895-516e-889a-c360066eca77" :answer/id #uuid "9abfb29c-8895-516e-889a-c360066eca77"] ["answer-9abfb29c-8895-516e-889a-c360066eca77" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9abfb29c-8895-516e-889a-c360066eca77" :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3"] ["answer-f2a48784-09b7-59a1-983c-3e13d504d2b6" :answer